From 8c12778feff90cbbeba21add485e9e2b156ca577 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Uwe Augustin <Info@FlapsOnline.de>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 21:37:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] SystemdConfigPanel: say why a service could not be
 started

Turning on a service whose start failed only made its row go back to
"Off" after a moment, without a word about the reason.  Two cases look
like that.  If a unit the service requires cannot be started, systemd
ends the job with "dependency" and never runs the service.  If the
service is of type "simple", the job ends with "done" as soon as the
program runs, even when the program gives up a moment later, for
instance because a device or another daemon it needs is missing.  Both
results were ignored.

The job result is now passed on by the systemd helpers, and after a
start or restart the panel watches the unit for a moment.  If it is not
running, the message names the reason: the required units that are not
running, the exit code or signal of the program, or what systemd
reports, followed by the last lines the journal has for the unit since
the start, which is usually where the program itself says what is
wrong.  The unit is loaded and referenced before the start, because
systemd unloads an inactive unit nobody refers to and would forget the
result of its program.

A service is now enabled for the next boot only if it actually runs;
before, a failed start still enabled it, so it failed again at every
boot.
